  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry conditions through midweek, with rain \nchances late week.\n\nA larger amplitude upper level shortwave trough is currently\nprogressing across the Mid-Atlantic states today today. A cold \nfront in association with this upper level trough passed through\nthe area during the overnight hours, which has lead to much\ndrier air advecting into the region. Given the forecasted \nprogressive nature of the trough, upper level ridging looks to \nbuild over the region by this evening. As a result, temperatures\nlook to continue to remain elevated in the low to mid 80s \nacross the mountains, and mid to upper 80s in the Piedmont. By \nthe end of the work week, daytime high temperatures look to \nincrease into the mid to upper 80s west of the Blue Ridge, and \nlow to mid 90s across the Piedmont. Dewpoint temperatures look \nto drop into the mid 50s to low 60s across the area for the next\ncouple of days, which will make for much more comfortable \nweather across the region through the middle of this week; \nhowever, low to mid 60s dewpoints look to return by the end of \nthe work week as southerly flow advects moisture back into the \nregion. \n\nEmbedded upper level shortwave disturbances rounding the top of\nthe upper level ridge looks to bring low rain chances back into\nthe forecast by Thursday for primarily mountain locations. The \naforementioned upper level ridge looks to possibly break down by\nnext weekend, with no significant rain chances expected prior \nto this.",
